findings=[
 {"ref":"new",
  "title":"A donation is shared by whoever holds in-range liquidity at the instant of the call, and core has no statement that keeps liquidity added moments earlier from collecting it",
  "chainIds":ALL,"lens":"L1","assumptions":["A5"],"effect":"siphon","actor":"unprivileged","severity":"low",
  "difference":"None in the code path, which is the same on every network. What differs per network is how easily an ordinary account can place its own calls directly around another party's donation: inside one transaction when the donation can be triggered by any caller, or in neighbouring transactions where pending transactions are visible and ordering can be bought. This unit did not establish the ordering rules of the 21 networks and names that as an open lead.",
  "mechanism":"1. donate(key, amount0, amount1) debits the caller and raises feeGrowthGlobal by amount * 2^128 / liquidity, where liquidity is the pool's active liquidity at slot0.tick when the call runs (src/PoolManager.sol:256-276, src/libraries/Pool.sol:466-480). There is no minimum holding time, no snapshot and no vesting. 2. A position added before the call earns its share liquidity / total through the ordinary fee growth path when it is next touched (Pool.sol:184-192, Position.sol:92-97) and can be removed right after. Adding and removing liquidity is open to any account and costs only the rounding of the amounts. 3. A second effect makes the set of recipients movable. When a zeroForOne swap ends exactly on the price of an initialized tick n, the tick is crossed and slot0.tick becomes n - 1 while the price is that of n (Pool.sol:413-432). A swap with sqrtPriceLimitX96 equal to the price of tick n does this on purpose. A donation made in that state goes to positions that cover tick n - 1, and positions whose lower bound is n receive nothing. 4. Spearbit 5.1.1 and Certora M-02 describe these two effects, both at medium. The recorded responses are comments (IPoolManager.sol:150-155, Pool.sol:409-412, Pool.sol:80-82). No statement in src closes either one. 5. Read-only log queries show donate in use: 108 Donate events in 5,000 blocks on Ethereum, 138 in 100,000 blocks on Robinhood Chain, 304 on Elysium and 7 on Arbitrum One. In the four transactions I opened, one per network, the donor is a contract and the same transaction also swaps through the pool that receives the donation.",
  "impact":"The donor's funds reach other accounts than the liquidity providers they were meant for, and the long standing providers of that pool lose the share. The amount at risk is one donation at a time. The sampled donations are small: 8.0e12 units of currency0 on Ethereum, 11,230 units of currency1 on Arbitrum One, 5.11e18 units of an 18 decimal currency on Robinhood Chain. A donation that is funded by the swap that triggers it gives a caller who triggers it only its own money back, so the loss arises where the donated amount was accrued earlier or where another party's swap can be bracketed. I did not find a donor contract that pays out an earlier accrued balance on a public call, and I did not look for one beyond the four samples.",
  "preconditions":"Some party donates to a pool. The attacker holds or can borrow both currencies for the length of the capture and can run its liquidity change directly before the donation: in the same transaction when the donating call is open to any caller, otherwise through transaction ordering on that network.",
  "mitigations":"Donors should not donate amounts that are worth capturing in one call. A hook that distributes fees can pay them over time or require that liquidity was present before the block of the donation, and can check both slot0.tick and the price before donating, as the comment at Pool.sol:409-412 advises. Core could not close this without storing per position time data, which the reports do not ask for.",
  "coreRefs":["src/PoolManager.sol:256-276","src/libraries/Pool.sol:466-480","src/libraries/Pool.sol:409-432","src/interfaces/IPoolManager.sol:149-155"],
  "sources":ALL_SRC,"evidence":"primary"},
 {"ref":"F-014",
  "title":"Spearbit 5.4.14 records a lock on sync as a fix and the source has none; because unlock is open to every caller the missing lock gives an attacker nothing new",
  "chainIds":ALL,"lens":"L2","assumptions":["A1"],"effect":"degrade","actor":"unprivileged","severity":"info",
  "difference":"None. The statement concerns the source text and holds on every network that keeps transient storage for exactly one transaction.",
  "mechanism":"Spearbit 5.4.14 warns that sync can be called outside an unlock and that a recipient of a native take gets control of the flow. The recorded response names two changes: a lock added to sync, and sync no longer reverting and accepting the native currency. The second is in the text: sync overwrites the synced currency, and sync(address(0)) clears it (src/PoolManager.sol:279-288). The first is not: sync has no onlyWhenUnlocked modifier, the interface comment still lists only initialize and updateDynamicLPFee as callable while locked (src/interfaces/IPoolManager.sol:111), and test/Sync.t.sol:36-41 calls sync while locked. I checked what the lock would have prevented. onlyWhenUnlocked tests one global flag (PoolManager.sol:96-99) and unlock is open to any caller (PoolManager.sol:104-114), so an account that wants a sync to be pending for later code in the same transaction can open its own unlock, call sync inside it and return with no delta. With or without the modifier the same state is reachable. What a pending sync can do is unchanged from F-014 and F-071: a later settle with value reverts with NonzeroNativeValue unless its caller syncs first (PoolManager.sol:353-356), and fee collection of that token reverts (ProtocolFees.sol:49-52). Spearbit's remaining warning also stands: tokens sent after sync(X) and followed by sync(Y) without a settle are credited to nobody. I looked for a way to claim such a surplus later and found none, because a later sync records the balance with the surplus in it and take lowers the balance only against an equal debit (PoolManager.sol:285-286 and 291-297).",
  "impact":"No funds can be taken through this difference. Routers that settle native value without their own sync can be made to revert by code that runs earlier in the same transaction, as F-071 records. A user who syncs one token, pays, and then syncs another without settling loses the payment to the contract for good.",
  "preconditions":"The attacker's code runs earlier in the same transaction as the victim's settlement, which needs a shared transaction such as a bundle, a batch or a callback.",
  "mitigations":"Integrators should call sync immediately before every payment, for native value as well, as the comment at PoolManager.sol:348 says. The interface comment at IPoolManager.sol:111 should name sync.",
  "coreRefs":["src/PoolManager.sol:279-288","src/PoolManager.sol:96-114","src/PoolManager.sol:349-364","src/interfaces/IPoolManager.sol:111"],
  "sources":[{"url":"docs/security/audits/DRAFT_Spearbit_audit_core.pdf: finding 5.4.14","supports":"the response lists a lock added to sync and a later change that makes sync override and accept the native currency, and Spearbit notes the unaccounted transfer when two syncs follow each other"},
             {"url":"src/PoolManager.sol:279","supports":"sync is declared external with no modifier"},
             {"url":"src/PoolManager.sol:104","supports":"unlock has no access check, so any caller can make the manager unlocked"},
             {"url":"test/Sync.t.sol:36","supports":"the repository's own test calls sync twice while the manager is locked and expects success"}],
  "evidence":"primary"}]
leads=[
 {"chainIds":ALL,"lens":"L6","question":"For each of the 21 networks, can an ordinary account place one transaction directly before and one directly after a pending donate or fee distributing swap of another party, and which hook or distributor contracts that call donate hold an earlier accrued balance that any caller can make them pay out? Start with the Donate senders on Ethereum, Robinhood Chain, Elysium and Arbitrum One listed in cycles/007/core-code-audit-walk-b.work/raw/donate_logs.json.","why":"Spearbit 5.1.1 and Certora M-02 are open in the source by decision. The loss is real only where a donation is not funded by the call that triggers it, or where another party's donation can be bracketed. Four networks show donations today and this unit read only four transactions.","priority":"medium"},
 {"chainIds":ALL,"lens":"L1","question":"In a build of this checkout with solc 0.8.26 and via_ir, does the generated code clean the bytes4 returned by ParseBytes.parseSelector before the comparison at src/libraries/Hooks.sol:152 and the uint24 returned by parseFee before it is written to Pool.SwapParams at src/PoolManager.sol:209-215?","why":"These are the two places where the fix for Certora L-03 and ABDK CVF-44 rests on the compiler and not on a mask in the text. If the selector comparison used raw words, a correct hook would be rejected, which would show at once in tests, so the risk is low. It belongs with the open build leads L-483 and L-016.","priority":"low"},
 {"chainIds":ALL,"lens":"L5","question":"What did the change that Certora cites as the partial fix of I-01 (pull request 786) add, and is that statement still in src? The report gives the number and no description.","why":"It is the one fix among the three reports that this unit could not map to a line. If it was a guard that limits who may open deltas during an unlock, its absence would matter to integrators that make untrusted calls inside an unlock.","priority":"low"}]
leadResults=[{"leadId":"L-482","outcome":"finding","answer":"Done for the Certora, Spearbit and ABDK drafts. Certora: 8 findings and the property tables. Spearbit: 36 findings. ABDK: CVF-1 to CVF-50 one by one and CVF-51 to CVF-176 in five groups. Every fix that the reports describe is present in src except two, both already known and neither usable for a gain: the lock on sync (Spearbit 5.4.14, see the F-014 entry) and one comment line of Spearbit 5.4.17. Open by decision of the authors: donation capture (Spearbit 5.1.1 with Certora M-02, new low finding), the per tick liquidity cap (Certora L-02, F-038), the unreachable minimum price (Certora L-01), reverts caused by untrusted callees (Certora I-01), missing events, and unbounded copying of revert data (Spearbit 5.2.5). No finding of the three reports names a property of a chain. Two touch a token or the native asset: Spearbit 5.3.10 assumes token supplies below 2^128, which the int128 casts enforce in code, and Spearbit 5.4.14 notes that a native payout hands control to the recipient (A3). The closing statements rely on A10 for the bit operations and on A1 for the delta counter and the synced currency, and on the compiler for two cleanups. All numeric constants named by the reports were recomputed: 20 TickMath multipliers, both price bounds, the logarithm error bounds through a sweep of all 1,774,545 ticks, both BitMath tables, the per tick cap for all spacings, the four transient slots and seven selectors. The OpenZeppelin and Trail of Bits reports and the hook permissions note belong to core-code-audit-walk-a."}]
